Lately I've been learning more about nutrition and my main takeaway is that eating healthy is surprisingly hard. Modern food is loitered with culinary footguns.
+ Almost every culture's cuisine has too much sodium. It's often from just a few sources like soy sauce and soups, but resteraunt food is generally pretty salty. 
+ American cuisine has way too much sugar. Americans eat 120 grams of sugar a day. For context, 2nd place only eats 90 grams, and the reccomended maximum is around 30 grams. Blame sauces and desserts.
+ "Staple" culinary dishes are often the _least_ healthy item from that cuisine. There are some obvious examples, like Butter Chicken or excessively sweetend Thai Curry, but it's often subtle. Did you know a single roll of Sushi has 15 grams of sugar? It's in the rice! Just two rolls singlehandedly get you to the daily sugar reccomendaiton. That might make you think Japanese people consume too much sugar, but sushi is not eaten that often there, many eat it less then 6 times a year.
+ American Processed Food is absurdly unhealthy. The reason is American food has historically (and still is) less regulated then European food. In a nutshell, America assumes additives are safe until prove otherwise, Europe assumes they're unsafe until proven safe. This has led to a variety of questionable things being put into our food...
	- Dyes which require warning labels or are outright banned in other countries.
	- Growth horomones, which has led to American Beef, Pork, and Poultry being outright banned in many nations. Beef in particular is banned in 160 countries. 160!
	- American Bread is banned in many places due to the additives it uses. It's also legally classified as cake in some parts of Europe.
	- Excess salt, sugar, and fat. These three ingredients are what is known as "The Holy Trifecta". It's a recipe for long shelf-life _only American ingenuinity_ could have devised. It's a pretty major contributor to the Obesity Epidemic.
	
Needless to say, it's pretty cooked. To combat this, I'm trying to avoid sugary and staple foods. I'm trying to eat something closer to the daily fare of people in other nations, as opposed to the stuff they have when they're celebrating. I also try to make my snacks as simple and natural as possible: berries, yogurt, hummus, etc. 

// One strategy is to look at what our ancestors were eating, since they were pretty healthy. However, it's hard to use that insight to figure out how to eat healthy today. First of all, you're probably a descendant of several different tribes who ate different diets and had different exercise regimens. Second, we have diverged from them a bit (at the very least the ability to digest seaweed and milk are pretty new!), it's unclear if their diet is still a good idea. Lastly, it's not even clear their diet was optimal for them (although they probably adapted to it) as they may have been resource constrained. 

// Another strategy is to just do a bunch of studies. This at least gives us high confidence some things are bad in excess (sugar, salt). We also know some "nutritional principles". Eating sugar with fiber (like it's natural food in fruit) is good for you, it keeps the blood-sugar spike down. Keep your gut filled with a diverse set of healthy microbes, it enables the breakdown of a variety of nutrients (including much of the nutritional content of seaweed), and it ensures your food cravings are in your interest, instead of a particular bacteria's. As an extreme example, there have been many cases where someone Obese was found to have _a single_ variety of bacteria in their gut, practically posessing them to make them eat the same unhealthy foods. That being said, there's no clear guideline

